          {
            "TOCHeading": "Metabolism/Metabolites",
            "Description": "Metabolites are substances made or used when the body breaks down food, drugs or chemicals, or its own tissue (for example, fat or muscle tissue). This process is called metabolism and makes energy and the materials needed for growth, reproduction, and maintaining health. Metabolism also helps get rid of toxic substances.",
            "Information": [
              {
                "ReferenceNumber": 24,
                "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                "Reference": [
                  "WHO; Environ Health Criteria 78: Dithiocarbamate Pesticides, Ethylenethiourea and Propylenethiourea (1988). Available from, as of March 4, 2010: https://www.inchem.org/pages/ehc.html"
                ],
                "ExtendedReference": [
                  {
                    "Citation": "WHO; Environ Health Criteria 78: Dithiocarbamate Pesticides, Ethylenethiourea and Propylenethiourea (1988). Available from, as of March 4, 2010: https://www.inchem.org/pages/ehc.html",
                    "Matched": {
