            "Description": "This section lists the chemical's safety and hazard characteristics, such as the explosive/flammable limits, critical temperature and pressure, exposure limits, etc.",
            "Section": [
              {
                "TOCHeading": "Physical Dangers",
                "Description": "The possibility of physical injury or sickness that could result in grave physical harm or death.  An example of physical dangers is fire/explosion due to raw materials.",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 25,
                    "Name": "",
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Dust explosion possible if in powder or granular form, mixed with air."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "TOCHeading": "Chemical Dangers",
                "Description": "Chemical dangers encompass physical and health hazards caused by exposure to toxic substances, corrosives, or flammables. They enter the body via inhalation, ingestion, injection, or skin absorption, leading to acute injuries like burns or chronic issues like cancer and organ damage.",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 25,
                    "Name": "",
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Decomposes on heating and on burning. This produces toxic fumes including carbon dioxide, nitrogen oxides and sulfur oxides. Unstable to light and moisture.",
                          "Markup": [
