                  {
                    "TOCHeading": "Lethal Dose",
                    "Description": "Lethal dose values describe the amount of a chemical expected to cause death in test subjects under specified exposure conditions. These values are commonly expressed as dose-based toxicity endpoints such as the median lethal dose (LD50), which represents the estimated dose expected to cause death in 50% of the test subjects. Other endpoints include LD0 (no observed deaths) and LD100 (death in all test subjects).",
                    "Information": [
                      {
                        "ReferenceNumber": 16,
                        "Value": {
                          "ExternalTableName": "collection=toxvaldb&kind=^LD$"
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "TOCHeading": "Effect Concentration",
                    "Description": "Effect concentration values describe the concentration of a chemical associated with observed biological or toxicological effects in test subjects. These concentration-based endpoints include Lowest Observed Effect Concentration (LOEC), No Observed Effect Concentration (NOEC), Lowest Observed Adverse Effect Concentration (LOAEC), and No Observed Adverse Effect Concentration (NOAEC), which represent concentrations associated with observed or unobserved effects or adverse effects under specified exposure conditions.",
                    "Information": [
                      {
                        "ReferenceNumber": 16,
                        "Value": {
                          "ExternalTableName": "collection=toxvaldb&kind=^EC$"
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "TOCHeading": "Effect Level",
                    "Description": "Effect level values describe the dose or exposure level of a chemical associated with observed biological or toxicological effects in test subjects. These dose-based endpoints include Low Effect Level (LEL), No Effect Level (NEL), Lowest Observed Effect Level (LOEL), No Observed Effect Level (NOEL), Lowest Observed Adverse Effect Level (LOAEL), and No Observed Adverse Effect Level (NOAEL), which represent exposure levels associated with observed or unobserved effects or adverse effects under specified exposure conditions.",
                    "Information": [
                      {
                        "ReferenceNumber": 16,
                        "Value": {
                          "ExternalTableName": "collection=toxvaldb&kind=^EL$"
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "TOCHeading": "Screening Level",
                    "Description": "Screening levels are health- or environment-based threshold values used to identify chemical concentrations or doses that may warrant additional evaluation or risk assessment. Examples include Ecological Screening Levels (ESLs), Soil Screening Levels (SSLs), Health-Based Screening Levels (HBSLs), and Maximum Contaminant Levels (MCLs) for drinking water.",
                    "Information": [
                      {
                        "ReferenceNumber": 16,
                        "Value": {
                          "ExternalTableName": "collection=toxvaldb&kind=^SL$"
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "TOCHeading": "Reference and Risk Values",
                    "Description": "Reference and risk values are derived toxicity benchmarks used to evaluate potential human health risks associated with chemical exposure. These values are typically developed from experimental toxicity data together with uncertainty or safety factors. Examples include Reference Dose (RfD), Reference Concentration (RfC), Derived No-Effect Level (DNEL), and other health-based guidance or risk assessment values.",
                    "Information": [
                      {
                        "ReferenceNumber": 16,
                        "Value": {
                          "ExternalTableName": "collection=toxvaldb&kind=^RRV$"
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "TOCHeading": "Military Exposure Guidelines (MEGs)",
                    "Description": "Military Exposure Guidelines (MEGs) are established by the Department of Defense (DoD) to evaluate and manage chemical and environmental health risks during deployments. MEGs help determine how chemical exposures in air, water, and soil could impact a service member's health and mission readiness",
                    "Information": [
                      {
                        "ReferenceNumber": 16,
                        "Value": {
                          "ExternalTableName": "collection=toxvaldb&kind=^MEG$"
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "TOCHeading": "Medium-Specific Concentration",
                    "Description": "Medium-specific concentration (MSC) is a chemical concentration in a specific medium (e.g., air, water, soil) that is considered to be safe for human health or the environment.  MSCs are used in risk assessments to evaluate potential risks from chemical exposures in different media.  The MSCs for a chemical can be derived from various sources, such as regulatory standards, toxicity data, and risk assessment models.",
