              {
                "TOCHeading": "ICSC Environmental Data",
                "Description": "Environmental effects of this chemical, provided by the International Labour Organization's (ILO's) International Chemical Safety Cards (ICSC).",
                "URL": "https://www.ilo.org/safework/info/publications/WCMS_113134/lang--en/index.htm",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 25,
                    "Name": "",
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "The substance is very toxic to aquatic organisms. The substance may cause long-term effects in the aquatic environment. This substance does enter the environment under normal use. Great care, however, should be taken to avoid any additional release, for example through inappropriate disposal."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "TOCHeading": "Environmental Fate/Exposure Summary",
                "Description": "This section summarizes the environmental fate/exposure of this chemical, including the natural and artificial sources of the chemical as a pollutant, its environmental fate, biodegradation, abiotic degradation, bioconcentration, etc.",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 24,
                    "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Zineb's former production in the US may have resulted in its release to the environment through various waste streams; its former use in the US as a fungicide resulted in its direct release to the environment. If released to air, a vapor pressure of <7.5X10-8 mm Hg at 20 °C indicates zineb is expected to exist solely in the particulate phase. Particulate-phase zineb will be removed from the atmosphere by wet or dry deposition. Zineb contains chromophores that absorb at wavelengths >290 nm and therefore may be susceptible to direct photolysis by sunlight. If released to soil, zineb is expected to have low to moderate mobility based upon a Koc values of 308-1159. Volatilization from moist soil surfaces is not expected to be an important fate process since zineb is a zinc salt. Zineb field half-lives range from 23-43 days. If released into water, zineb is expected to adsorb to suspended solids and sediment based upon the Koc values. Surface water mixed with sediment produced 70% cumulative radio-labeled carbon dioxide after 50 days incubation, suggesting that biodegradation may be an important fate process in water. Volatilization from water surfaces is not expected to be an important fate process since zineb is a zinc salt. An estimated BCF of 3 suggests the potential for bioconcentration in aquatic organisms is low. Hydrolysis may be an important environmental fate process as indicated by half-lives in relation to pH of 9 minutes at pH 3.8, 6.5 hrs at pH 5.7, 96 hrs at pH 7.0, and 405 hrs at pH 8.0. Occupational exposure to zineb may occur through inhalation of dust and dermal contact with this compound at workplaces where zineb was formerly produced or used. Monitoring data indicate that the general population may be exposed to zineb via ingestion of food treated with zineb and dermal contact with fungicide products containing zineb, from sources other than the US. (SRC)",
                          "Markup": [
                            {
                              "Start": 0,
                              "Length": 5,
                              "URL": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Zineb",
                              "Type": "PubChem Internal Link",
                              "Extra": "CID-5284484"
                            },
