              },
              {
                "TOCHeading": "Environmental Biodegradation",
                "Description": "This section provides information on environmental biodegradation for this compound. Biodegradation is the breakdown of organic matter by microorganisms, such as bacteria and fungi.  An important measure of biodegradability of wastewater is the biochemical oxygen demand (BOD), which is the amount of dissolved oxygen (DO) needed (i.e. demanded) by aerobic biological organisms to break down organic material present in a given water sample at certain temperature over a specific time period. ",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 24,
                    "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                    "Reference": [
                      "(1) Verschueren K; Handbook of Environmental Data on Organic Chemicals. 3rd ed. NY, NY: Van Nostrand Reinhold p. 1911 (1996)",
                      "(2) NITE; Chemical Risk Information Platform (CHRIP). Biodegradation and Bioconcentration. Ver 2006.01.30 Updated. National Institute of Technology and Evaluation. Tokyo, Japan. Chemcial. 12122-67-7. Available from the database query page at https://www.safe.nite.go.jp/english/kizon/KIZON_start_hazkizon.html as of Dec 2009.",
                      "(3) Freitag D et al; Chemosphere 14: 1589-616 (1985)",
                      "(4) Rajagopal BS et al; Res Rev 93: 1-199 (1984)",
                      "(5) Bruggemann R, Bartel HG; J Chem Inf Comput Sci 39: 211-7 (1999)"
                    ],
                    "ExtendedReference": [
                      {
                        "Citation": "(1) Verschueren K; Handbook of Environmental Data on Organic Chemicals. 3rd ed. NY, NY: Van Nostrand Reinhold p. 1911 (1996)"
                      },
                      {
                        "Citation": "(2) NITE; Chemical Risk Information Platform (CHRIP). Biodegradation and Bioconcentration. Ver 2006.01.30 Updated. National Institute of Technology and Evaluation. Tokyo, Japan. Chemcial. 12122-67-7. Available from the database query page at https://www.safe.nite.go.jp/english/kizon/KIZON_start_hazkizon.html as of Dec 2009."
                      },
                      {
                        "Citation": "(3) Freitag D et al; Chemosphere 14: 1589-616 (1985)"
                      },
                      {
                        "Citation": "(4) Rajagopal BS et al; Res Rev 93: 1-199 (1984)"
                      },
                      {
                        "Citation": "(5) Bruggemann R, Bartel HG; J Chem Inf Comput Sci 39: 211-7 (1999)"
                      }
                    ],
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "AEROBIC: The biodegradation of zineb in surface water was studied using radiolabeled carbon. Without sediment in the system, 6% cumulative radiolabeled carbon dioxide production was observed after 50 days incubation(1). Surface water mixed with sediment produced 70% cumulative radiolabeled carbon dioxide after 50 days incubation(1). Ethylene thiourea is the major metabolite in plants along with ethylene thiuram monosulfide and presumable thiuram disulfide and sulfur(1). Zineb, present at 100 mg/L, was not biodegraded in 4 weeks using an activated sludge inoculum at 30 mg/L in the Japanese MITI test(2). In a screening test using an activated sludge inoculum, 1.1% of the applied zineb was mineralized in 5 days(3). Zinc is released from the molecule by soil microorganisms to an appreciable extent(4). The biodegradation half-life has been reported as 30 days for zineb(5).",
                          "Markup": [
