              },
              {
                "TOCHeading": "Plant Concentrations",
                "Description": "Concentrations of this compound in plants.",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 24,
                    "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                    "Reference": [
                      "(1) Nash RG, Beal ML Jr; J Agric Food Chem 28: 322-30 (1980)",
                      "(2) Ripley BD, Simpson CM; Pestic Sci 8: 487-91 (1977)"
                    ],
                    "ExtendedReference": [
                      {
                        "Citation": "(1) Nash RG, Beal ML Jr; J Agric Food Chem 28: 322-30 (1980)"
                      },
                      {
                        "Citation": "(2) Ripley BD, Simpson CM; Pestic Sci 8: 487-91 (1977)"
                      }
                    ],
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Residues on tomato leaves were 50 ppm after spraying and declined with a half-life of 14 days(1). The decline of zineb residues after spraying to half its initial concentration was 7, 11, and 35 days for tomatoes, lettuce, and grapes, respectively(1). Immediately after spraying, zineb residues on pear foliage was about 350 ppm, declining 27 ppm/day in the following 9 days and then more slowly (about 4 ppm/day)(2). Bartlett pears sprayed twice with zineb, the second application coming 14 days after the first, contained 5.2 ppm of zineb",
                          "Markup": [
                            {
                              "Start": 113,
                              "Length": 5,
                              "URL": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/zineb",
