                    "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                    "Reference": [
                      "(1) NIOSH; NOES. National Occupational Exposure Survey conducted from 1981-1983. Estimated numbers of employees potentially exposed to specific agents by 2-digit standard industrial classification (SIC). Available from, as pf Dec 2009: https://www.cdc.gov/noes/"
                    ],
                    "ExtendedReference": [
                      {
                        "Citation": "(1) NIOSH; NOES. National Occupational Exposure Survey conducted from 1981-1983. Estimated numbers of employees potentially exposed to specific agents by 2-digit standard industrial classification (SIC). Available from, as pf Dec 2009: https://www.cdc.gov/noes/"
                      }
                    ],
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "NIOSH (NOES Survey 1981-1983) has statistically estimated that 3,205 workers (none of these were female) were potentially exposed to zineb in the US(1). However, the NOES Survey does not include farm workers. Occupational exposure to zineb may occur through inhalation of dust and dermal contact with this compound at workplaces where zineb was formally produced or used. Monitoring data indicate that the general population may be exposed to zineb via ingestion of food treated with zineb, and dermal contact with fungicide products containing zineb, from sources other than the US(SRC).",
                          "Markup": [
                            {
                              "Start": 133,
